From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dima Nikiforov <vnikiforov@berkeley.edu>
Date: Tue, 16 Sep 2026 09:20:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] perfetto: one merged timeline, one named track per hart

WHAT WAS BROKEN. `PerfettoReceiver` wrote `"pid": 0, "tid": 0` on every event it
emitted. In Chrome/Perfetto JSON a track IS the (pid, tid) pair carried on each
event, so two decoded traces could not be told apart even when concatenated by
hand: every slice claimed the same track and the two harts landed on top of each
other. A dual-hart capture was therefore two files, opened one at a time, with
the reader doing the alignment in their head -- which is exactly the part a
timeline is for. fpga/pynq-z2/docs/TACIT_MULTICORE.md section 8 recorded this as
the open gap: "The two traces are still two files with two `pid`s."

WHAT THIS ADDS is a second input mode and nothing else:

    ltrace-decoder --binary zephyr.elf --encoder rtl --to-txt --to-perfetto \
      --trace hart0/tacit.out:0:'hart 0 (big, MBP)' \
      --trace hart1/tacit.out:1:'hart 1 (LITTLE)'  \
      --merged-perfetto trace.merged.perfetto.json

Repeated flags rather than a manifest file, because the caller is a shell script
that already has the per-hart path in a loop variable:

    for h in 0 1; do ARGS+=(--trace "$RUN/hart$h/tacit.out:$h:${LABEL[$h]}"); done

is the whole integration. No temporary file to write, clean up, or keep in step
with the run; the paths stay visible in the command line the lab echoes; and
adding a third hart is one more loop iteration rather than a new file format.

FILE may not contain ':'; HART defaults to the argument's position; LABEL
defaults to "hart <N>" and is everything after the second colon, so it may
contain colons. HART becomes the Perfetto pid and tid, and LABEL is emitted as
the `"ph":"M"` `process_name` / `thread_name` metadata for that pair, so the UI
shows a NAMED track per hart. The decoder does not invent the names: only the
caller knows that hart 0 is the big core with the MBP datapath.

Each trace still writes its own `trace.txt` and `trace.perfetto.json`, next to
its encoded trace -- which is where the labs already keep them, so nothing that
read the per-hart files stops working. The merged file is an addition.

TIMESTAMPS ARE NOT TOUCHED. Nothing here rescales or re-bases anything; the
merged file carries the same raw `mcycle` values the per-hart files carry. That
is only meaningful because patches/0004-rocketchip-mcycle-free-run.patch made
both harts' `mcycle` free-run, and it is the caller's job to know that. Merging
traces from an unpatched bitstream produces two tracks millions of cycles apart,
correctly -- the decoder has no way to detect it and does not pretend to.

MEASURED, on the two dual-hart captures already on disk:

  out/rocket_tacit_pext_smp, merged, one axis:
    hart 0 (big, MBP)   tacit_sync_marker @8,971,943   mbp_body_hw    701 cycles
    hart 1 (LITTLE)     tacit_sync_marker @8,971,933   mbp_body_sw 20,924 cycles
  The two markers are 10 cycles apart and the two bodies sit side by side on one
  timeline, 29.8x wide apart, which is the picture the lab exists to produce.

SINGLE-TRACE BEHAVIOUR IS UNCHANGED, and that is checked rather than asserted.
`--encoded-trace` takes `PerfettoTrack::default()`: pid 0, tid 0, `label: None`,
and `label: None` suppresses the metadata events entirely, so the emitted bytes
are the ones the old code emitted. Re-decoding all five single traces in the
repo (samples/tacit's full boot, and both harts of both dual-hart captures)
produced `trace.perfetto.json` and `trace.txt` byte-identical to what was on
disk before this patch, and scripts/10_tacit_hello.sh still reports
541930 / 258319 / 793201 / 2372 against expected/tacit.json.

`--encoded-trace` became optional at the clap level, since `--trace` replaces
it; passing neither, or both, is a hard error with a message that says which.
The other nine backends each write one fixed file name into the current
directory, which cannot be right for more than one hart, so combining them with
`--trace` is refused rather than letting hart 1 silently overwrite hart 0.

`cargo test` covers the default track's pid/tid/metadata contract, the metadata
JSON exactly as serde_json emits it, the `traceEvents` framing with and without
metadata, and the four `--trace` spec parses.
